# Target C — audited §171 orbit-level atom/detour inequality and stronger explicit Type-II gap

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only Type-II route theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Dependencies:** audited §§142, 166, 168.

Section 166 charges one persistent train-direction atom against bounded-face endpoint capacity or outer-walk detour. Section 171 charges a complete three-direction `pi/3` atomic orbit before selecting an outer atom. This removes the factor-three loss on bounded-face capacity and, when combined with the explicit §168 train gap, improves the all-Type-II `q-k` floor.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
C_{146}:={4\over9}(\pi+s),
\qquad
g:=0.0004.
\tag{171.1}
\]

Along a Type-II subsequential limit write

\[
M=\lim {b\over D},
\qquad
B=\lim {Q+4f\over D},
\qquad
C=\lim {T-q/2\over D},
\tag{171.2}
\]

and

\[
\delta=\lim {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}=M-\pi\ge0.
\tag{171.3}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
B+\bigl(81M^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}
\ge {8\Delta_2\over C}.
}
\tag{171.4}
\]

This strictly strengthens the §166 one-atom inequality whenever `B>0`.

The explicit §168 lower bound

\[
\boxed{C\ge2s+g}
\tag{171.5}
\]

then yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+0.00137
=1.8208835379794446\ldots .
}
\tag{171.6}
\]

The safe scalar endpoint `0.00137` lies below the first zero `0.00137099214...` of the same coarse feasibility envelope.

---

## 171.1 One heavy atomic orbit

Let the empirical complete-train direction measures satisfy

\[
{T\over D}\to x,
\qquad
\mu_D\Rightarrow\mu.
\]

For every atom `phi` of `mu`, write its absolute `D`-normalized train mass as

\[
a_\phi=x\mu(\{\phi\}).
\]

Partition projective directions into the order-three orbits of

\[
\phi\mapsto\phi+{\pi\over3}\pmod\pi.
\]

For one orbit `r`, let

\[
A_r=\sum_{\phi\in r}a_\phi
\]

and let `E_r` be its limiting normalized strict-rhombus crossing mass. Audited §142 gives

\[
E_r\le{A_r^2\over4},
\qquad
\sum_r E_r=\Delta_2.
\tag{171.7}
\]

Put

\[
A=\sum_rA_r=x\sigma,
\qquad
R=\sup_rA_r.
\]

Then

\[
\Delta_2
\le {1\over4}\sum_rA_r^2
\le {R\over4}\sum_rA_r
={RA\over4}.
\tag{171.8}
\]

The §142 exposed-hull inequality `q/T<=2(1-sigma)` gives

\[
A=x\sigma
\le x-{1\over2}\lim {q\over D}
=C.
\tag{171.9}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{R\ge {4\Delta_2\over C}.}
\tag{171.10}
\]

No maximizing orbit need exist; a sequence of orbits whose masses tend to `R` is sufficient.

---

## 171.2 A heavy orbit forces an outer atom or bounded-face payment

Fix one atomic orbit `r`. It contains at most three directions. Choose disjoint shrinking projective neighborhoods of those directions whose boundaries are continuity sets for both the train-direction limit and the occurrence-counted outer-direction limit `nu` from §166.

Every stopped train has exactly two terminal face-side occurrences. The total bounded-face endpoint capacity over all directions is `Q+4f`. Hence after taking the source limit and then shrinking the neighborhoods,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\phi\in r}\nu(\{\phi\})
\ge (2A_r-B)_+.
}
\tag{171.11}
\]

Since the orbit has at most three directions, one of them is an outer atom of mass

\[
\boxed{
w_r\ge {(2A_r-B)_+\over3}.}
\tag{171.12}
\]

The hostile-audited §166 cosine-cusp lemma says every outer atom of mass `w` satisfies

\[
\delta\ge {w^3\over3M^2}.
\tag{171.13}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
(2A_r-B)_+^3\le81M^2\delta.
}
\tag{171.14}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
2A_r\le B+(81M^2\delta)^{1/3}.
\tag{171.15}
\]

Let `A_r->R` and use (171.10). This proves (171.4).

Singleton trains and bridges cause no multiplicity problem: both the §143 endpoint multigraph and the outer direction measure count physical face-side occurrences.

---

## 171.3 Exact scalar variables near the Type-II endpoint

Pass to a common Type-II subsequence on which

\[
t:=\lim {q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa:=\lim {k\over D},
\qquad
\varphi:=\lim {f\over D},
\qquad
\chi:=\lim {Q\over D}
\]

exist. The exact outer ledger gives

\[
\boxed{\chi=2t-\pi-\delta.}
\tag{171.16}
\]

Since `q/D=t+kappa` and `T=q+2f-k`,

\[
{T\over D}=t+2\varphi
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
C={t\over2}+2\varphi-{\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{171.17}
\]

Every bounded nonquadrilateral face contributes at least one unit to `Q`, so

\[
0\le\varphi\le\chi.
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
C\le {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{171.18}
\]

Also

\[
B=\chi+4\varphi.
\]

Solving (171.17) for `4varphi` and using (171.16),

\[
\boxed{
B=t+2C+\kappa-\pi-\delta.
}
\tag{171.19}
\]

These are exact normalized identities/inequalities on the chosen subsequence.

---

## 171.4 Reduce all endpoint feasibility to one scalar variable

Fix `t,delta,kappa`. In the feasibility margin associated to (171.4),

\[
B+(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta)^{1/3}-{8\Delta_2\over C},
\]

both the `2C` contribution to `B` and the term `-8Delta_2/C` increase with `C`. Therefore the largest possible margin uses the upper bound in (171.18). Substituting that bound into (171.19) gives

\[
B\le10t-5\pi-5\delta,
\tag{171.20}
\]

independently of `kappa`. The denominator in the negative term is largest when `kappa=0`, so the most favorable possible configuration has `kappa=0` as well.

Hence any feasible Type-II limit must satisfy, for some `delta>=0`,

\[
\boxed{
F_t(\delta):=
10t-5\pi-5\delta
+\bigl(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}
-{8\Delta_2\over {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta}
\ge0.
}
\tag{171.21}
\]

The explicit §168 gap (171.5) and (171.18) imply

\[
2s+g
\le {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
0\le\delta\le d_{max}(t):=
{ {9t\over2}-2\pi-(2s+g)\over2}.
}
\tag{171.22}
\]

Because `(9/2)C146-2pi=2s`, this is

\[
d_{max}(t)
={ {9\over2}(t-C_{146})-g\over2}.
\tag{171.23}
\]

---

## 171.5 Explicit contradiction at `C146+0.00137`

Let

\[
\varepsilon_0=0.00137,
\qquad
t_0=C_{146}+\varepsilon_0.
\]

For fixed `delta`, `F_t(delta)` is strictly increasing in `t`, since

\[
\partial_tF_t
=10+{36\Delta_2\over({9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta)^2}>0.
\tag{171.24}
\]

Thus it suffices to bound `F_{t_0}`.

At the endpoint,

\[
\boxed{
d_0=d_{max}(t_0)=0.0028825.}
\tag{171.25}
\]

For `0<delta<=d_0`, write

\[
K(\delta)=\bigl(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\bigr)^{1/3}.
\]

Then

\[
K'(\delta)
={81^{1/3}\over3}
(\pi+3\delta)(\pi+\delta)^{-1/3}\delta^{-2/3}.
\tag{171.26}
\]

Using `delta<=d_0`,

\[
K'(\delta)
\ge {81^{1/3}\over3}\pi(\pi+d_0)^{-1/3}d_0^{-2/3}
>152.69.
\tag{171.27}
\]

The denominator in (171.21) is at least `2s+g`. Hence

\[
\partial_\delta F_{t_0}
=-5+K'(\delta)
-{16\Delta_2\over({9t_0\over2}-2\pi-2\delta)^2}
>152.69-5-{16\Delta_2\over(2s+g)^2}
>143.6>0.
\tag{171.28}
\]

So `F_{t_0}` is strictly increasing on its entire feasible interval. Its maximum is at `delta=d_0`, where the denominator is exactly `2s+g`.

Direct outward-safe evaluation gives

\[
10t_0-5\pi-5d_0
=2.48645961184548\ldots,
\]

\[
K(d_0)=1.32165130742282\ldots,
\]

and

\[
{8\Delta_2\over2s+g}
=3.80845139543257\ldots .
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
F_{t_0}(d_0)
=-0.000340476164268\ldots<0.
}
\tag{171.29}
\]

This contradicts the necessary feasibility condition (171.21). Thus no Type-II survivor subsequence can have `t<=t_0`, proving (171.6).

The first zero of the exact one-variable endpoint envelope is approximately

\[
0.00137099214028
\]

above `C146`; only the round value `0.00137` is claimed.

---

## 171.6 Hostile-audit boundaries

The derivation was checked for the following failure modes.

1. **Orbit versus atom mass:** weighted Mantel is applied to entire `pi/3` orbits before selecting one of at most three outer atoms.
2. **Bounded endpoint charge:** (171.11) uses the entire global bounded-face capacity `B`, so no unproved directionwise face-capacity statement is inserted.
3. **Cusp coefficient:** the inherited §166 lemma is the hostile-audited `delta>=w^3/(3M^2)` form.
4. **Normalization:** `C=(T-q/2)/D`, not `T/D`; exact equations (171.16)–(171.19) were rederived from the current Type-II ledgers.
5. **Optimization directions:** the feasibility margin increases with `C`, decreases when `kappa` lowers the maximum available `C`, and increases with `t`; all relaxations maximize the chance of survival.
6. **Detour range:** the explicit §168 lower bound on `C` supplies the upper bound on `delta`; no zero-detour hypothesis is used.
7. **One-variable monotonicity:** (171.27) is a uniform lower bound on the cubic derivative, while the negative denominator derivative is bounded using `C>=2s+g`; the final margin is not a sampled optimizer.
8. **Source scope:** contact-isolated source points remain in the full source and do not enter the core train/rigidity kernel.

This theorem does not close Type II. It strengthens the quantitative endpoint floor and provides a reusable orbit-level capacity/detour inequality. The next structural target is to combine (171.4) with the exact five-currency hull-side conservation law, where negative hull-side correction is forced to reappear as outer-walk surplus.

**Disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. C-II and Level I remain open.